WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language:  en

00:00:05.830 --> 00:00:10.970
From the source, the water flows through channels
into the terraced gardens of Tipon.

00:00:10.970 --> 00:00:15.540
Built 600 years ago, it's an idyllic place.

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:25.440
300 people once lived here, explains a hydraulic
engineer who's worked here for decades.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:30.920
The ruin is situated three and a half thousand
meters above sea level and is considered a

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.660
masterpiece of water management.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:42.320
"Tipon was a huge ecological experiment, where
the pre-Hispanic people made use of nature

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:44.510
and especially water.

00:00:44.510 --> 00:00:50.080
It was used for rituals and ceremonies and
also for agriculture.

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:54.780
Specific hydraulic systems were developed
for these purposes - for example the channels,

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:57.760
the subterranean cisterns and the aquaducts."

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:06.320
The Incas succeeded in channelling the water
over a course of several hundred meters down

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:11.830
the hillside, without it gaining pace,
then sending it in various directions to irrigate

00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:15.060
their gardens -- and then on down into the
valley.

00:01:22.500 --> 00:01:28.400
A few kilometers to the west is Cusco, the capital of the Inca Empire before the Spanish conquest.

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:32.920
Today over 2 million tourists visit each year.

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:35.900
The city is thriving, its population booming.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.400
450,000 people live here.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:41.080
But it hasn't lost its unique atmosphere.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:43.740
The legacy of the Incas still survives.

00:01:48.840 --> 00:01:54.770
Even the course of the river Sapi, outside
the city limits, is a testament to Inca history.

00:01:54.770 --> 00:02:00.400
Like in Tipon, the water is channelled into
man-made basins to slow down the flow.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.060
That also helps remove debris from the water.

00:02:04.120 --> 00:02:06.860
But washed-up trash is the least of the problems.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:11.299
Much worse is the raw sewage from thousands
of toilets.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:14.260
The stench is overwhelming!

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:22.520
This engineer works for the Sedacusco water utility company, which aims to improve the situation.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:31.360
"Our goal is to decontaminate Cusco's water.

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.780
Including here in the river Sapi.

00:02:37.820 --> 00:02:42.700
The water here is filthy and it's our duty
to rid the river of toxins."

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:48.680
The problem is the many new housing developments
on the outskirts of the city.

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:51.230
There are fifty in the northwest alone.

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:56.000
All generate sewage from toilets, sinks, baths,
washing machines and dishwashers.

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.400
The river flows in underground channels to the neighbourhood of San Jeronimo

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.300
at the other end of the city.

00:03:06.280 --> 00:03:09.420
From there it flows into a sewage treatment
plant.

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:14.220
It's the pride of Cusco.

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:18.000
It's been undergoing renovation since 2014.

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:21.900
With the help of German consultants, it's
being made more efficient.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:31.700
"Working in Peru's most modern sewage treatment
plant is very gratifying.

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:34.410
It's also very challenging.

00:03:34.410 --> 00:03:38.880
Every day we investigate new technologies
and learn about how cities in other countries

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.540
are implementing them -- things that are new
to us in Peru."

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.840
Most of Peru's sewage ends up - unfiltered
- in rivers, lakes and the ocean.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:56.000
It's dangerously unhygienic and an environmental
nightmare.

00:04:02.240 --> 00:04:08.580
"Human waste generate approximately 75 kilos
of carbon dioxide per year per person.

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:12.560
If it isn't treated, it could soon be 400.

00:04:15.280 --> 00:04:21.700
This plant allows us to reduce human-generated
emissions to 20 kilos of carbon dioxide per year.

00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:27.240
You can smell the improvement.

00:04:28.380 --> 00:04:33.300
The fermentation gas that accrues in sewage
treatment plants is now filtered out more

00:04:33.309 --> 00:04:37.650
efficiently - so the stench is far less overwhelming.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:40.240
Much to the relief of the plant's neighbours.....

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.180
On Avenida Collana, for example.

00:04:45.780 --> 00:04:49.000
This shop owner has been living here for seven
years.

00:04:49.000 --> 00:04:51.380
The street used to be notorious.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.520
"When you came here by bus, the stop would
be announced as 'Buenos Aires' - 'good air'.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.380
That's what the neighbourhood was called.

00:05:04.380 --> 00:05:05.400
Noone would visit.

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:07.240
Even the buses didn't want to come here."

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.080
It was especially bad at midday.

00:05:16.700 --> 00:05:20.640
"In the past, when I would eat over there
with my mother-in-law, we thought at first

00:05:20.650 --> 00:05:23.150
that the stench came from the bathroom.

00:05:23.150 --> 00:05:26.180
And anyone who visited us would notice the
smell!

00:05:26.180 --> 00:05:29.660
We'd always tell them that it came from the
plant.

00:05:29.660 --> 00:05:31.220
It's much better now.

00:05:31.220 --> 00:05:32.920
It smells a bit.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:34.300
But it's nowhere near as bad."

00:05:38.750 --> 00:05:43.400
Delegations come from across the country to
visit the sewage treatment plant -- on average

00:05:43.409 --> 00:05:44.849
two a month.

00:05:44.849 --> 00:05:50.159
The stench has largely disappeared thanks
to the optimized biogas facility --

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:55.320
it prevents thousands of tonnes of greenhouse
gases from being released into the atmosphere.

00:05:57.460 --> 00:06:02.900
"We're in the process of deciding a framework
for the biogas team.

00:06:02.900 --> 00:06:09.020
The plan is for Cusco to convert 2000 cubic
meters of gas into electricity by the end of the year."

00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:16.660
The plant will soon be completely self-sufficient.

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:21.560
The water company will save over 20,000 euros
in electricity costs per month.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:27.440
The only question that remains is - how did
the Incas deal with sewage?

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:31.600
"This is the bedroom.

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:38.340
The toilet, the bathroom, was probably adjacent."

00:06:40.340 --> 00:06:41.360
"The bathroom?"

00:06:45.800 --> 00:06:50.420
"We assume it was the bathroom on the basis
of the floor plan and the ceramics we found.

00:06:54.300 --> 00:07:00.200
Researchers have identified layers of organic
material on pots, with layers of sand between them.

00:07:03.940 --> 00:07:07.060
The Incas probably used them as chamber pots."

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.399
The cat litter principle.

00:07:12.399 --> 00:07:16.680
Perhaps the Incas valued their water supply
too highly to befoul it.

